<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Lullaby · 1818 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Silent Night</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="de">Stille Nacht, heilige Nacht</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Christmas carol first sung with a guitar in a village church in Austria in 1818, now sung in about 300 languages.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Joseph Mohr</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Franz Xaver Gruber</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Catholic parish of Oberndorf, Salzburg; later Christians worldwide</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Oberndorf bei Salzburg, Austria</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>words 1816, music 1818; first performed on Christmas Eve 1818</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Lullaby</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Joseph Mohr (1816; he died in 1848); music by Franz Xaver Gruber (1818))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>Joseph Mohr, a young Catholic priest, wrote the poem &quot;Stille Nacht&quot; in 1816, in the aftermath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, at Mariapfarr in the Lungau region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, his father's home town, where he worked as an assistant priest. In 1817 he came to Oberndorf, a village on the Salzach river, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>.</p><p>On Christmas Eve 1818 Mohr brought the words to Franz Xaver Gruber, schoolmaster and organist in the nearby village of Arnsdorf, and asked him to compose a melody with guitar accompaniment for that night's mass; flooding of the river had possibly damaged the church organ. The song was first performed that night in the Nikolauskirche, Oberndorf's parish church. The church was later destroyed by repeated flooding and replaced by the Silent-Night-Chapel. The story, told in many films, that the organ had broken down comes from a fictional tale published in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in the 1930s.</p><p>According to Gruber, the organ builder Karl Mauracher, who serviced the Oberndorf instrument, took the song home with him to the Zillertal. There two travelling families of folk singers, the Strassers and the Rainers, took it into their shows. The Rainers were singing it around Christmas 1819, once performed it before an audience that included Franz I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I of Russia</a>, and gave its first performance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City in 1839. By the 1840s it was well known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> and reportedly a favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV of Prussia</a>. Over these years the melody changed slightly: Gruber's original was a moderato tune in 6/8 time with a siciliana rhythm, while the version sung today is a slow, meditative lullaby or pastorale. The first edition was published by Friese in 1833 in a collection of Four Genuine Tyrolean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a>.</p><p>For a long time the song was attributed to more famous Austrian composers, including Haydn, Mozart and Schubert. In 1995 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in Mohr's handwriting, dated by researchers to about 1820, was discovered; it showed that Mohr wrote the words in 1816 and Gruber composed the music in 1818. It is the earliest surviving manuscript and the only one in Mohr's hand.</p><p>In 1859 John Freeman Young, an Episcopal priest serving at Trinity Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> City, published the English translation most often sung today, made from three of Mohr's six verses. The carol has been translated into about 300 languages. It was first recorded in 1905 and has more than 137,000 known recordings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared it an intangible cultural heritage in 2011.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A quiet Christmas night song about the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>: the newborn child sleeps under the watch of his parents while shepherds hear the news from angels. Its mood is calm, tender and devotional, close to a cradle song.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks sg-blocks-2"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Stille Nacht! Heil’ge Nacht!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Gottes Sohn! O! wie lacht</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Lieb’ aus Deinem göttlichen Mund,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Da uns schlägt die rettende Stund;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Jesus! in Deiner Geburth!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Jesus in Deiner Geburth!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Stille Nacht! Heil’ge Nacht!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die der Welt Heil gebracht,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Aus des Himmels goldenen Höh’n,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Uns der Gnade Fülle läßt seh’n</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Jesum in Menschengestalt!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Jesum in Menschen-Gestalt!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Stille Nacht! Heil’ge Nacht!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wo sich heut alle Macht</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Väterlicher Liebe ergoß,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und als Bruder Huldvoll umschloß</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Jesus die Völker der Welt!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Jesus die Völker der Welt!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Stille Nacht! Heil'ge Nacht!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Lange schon uns bedacht,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Als der Herr vom Grimme befreyt,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">In der Väter urgrauer Zeit</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Aller Welt Schonung verhieß!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Aller Welt Schonung verhieß!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Stille Nacht! Heil’ge Nacht!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Hirten erst kundgemacht</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Durch der Engel „Hallelujah!“</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Tönt es laut bey Ferne und Nah</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">„Jesus der Retter ist da!“</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">„Jesus der Retter ist da!“</span></p></div></div><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Silent night, holy night,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>All is calm, all is bright</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>'Round yon Virgin Mother and Child.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Holy Infant so tender and mild,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sleep in heavenly peace,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sleep in heavenly peace,</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Silent night, holy night,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Darkness, flies and all is light;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Shepherds hear the angels sing:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>'Alleluia! hail the King!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Jesus the Saviour is here,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Jesus the Saviour is here.'</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Holy night, peaceful night,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Child of heaven, O how bright</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thou didst smile when thou wast born;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Blessed was that happy morn,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Full of heavenly joy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Full of heavenly joy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Silent night, holy night,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Guiding Star, O lend thy light;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>See the eastern wise men bring</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Gifts and homage to our King!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Jesus the Saviour is here,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Jesus the Saviour is here.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Silent night, holy night,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Wondrous Star, O lend thy light!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>With the angels let us sing</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Alleluia to our King!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Jesus our Saviour is here,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Jesus our Saviour is here.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-e" data-say>Amen.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Joseph Mohr (words 1816), Franz Xaver Gruber (music 1818). Text from <a href="https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/Weyhnachts-Lied" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>English: English words after John Freeman Young (1859), as printed in The Army and Navy Hymnal (1920). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Army_and_Navy_Hymnal%2FHymns%2FSilent_Night%2C_Holy_Night" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
